In this lesson, we'll look at designing a classification algorithm, which is really just a fancy way of saying a flowchart that sorts shapes into the right group. The trick is to pick good questions, put them in the right order, and then test your algorithm to make sure it works. First, list every category your shapes need to land in, so nothing gets missed. Second, pick questions that actually split your shapes into smaller groups. A bad question is one where every shape answers the same way, because it tells you nothing. Third, put your most powerful question first, the one that cuts the group in half fastest. Fourth, test it. Run shapes you already know through your algorithm and check they end up where they should. If one ends up in the wrong box, fix the question or the order. Oh, and a quick tip from the warning box: always test on shapes that sit on the boundary, like a square, which is both a rectangle and a rhombus. That's where algorithms usually break. Now let's see it in action with the triangle and quadrilateral examples.